From bd14ab777de7a9119963b552f9e527a8c436386e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: mesa-nvk-horizon <noreply@invalid>
Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 19:50:01 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 78/78] nouveau/vk: nvkmd_horizon, an unmap waits for the work
 that can still reach it

mesa-nvk-horizon: Phase 4 item 5 (bind / unbind)
Why: on every other nvkmd backend an unbind goes through the kernel's
 VM_BIND queue and comes out ordered after the channel's in-flight work.
 NVGPU has no such queue, and this backend said so in a comment and then
 drew the wrong conclusion from it: "the mapping is in place before this
 call returns, so anything submitted afterwards sees it". That holds for
 a bind. For an unmap the same immediacy is the opposite of safe --
 NVGPU_AS_IOCTL_UNMAP_BUFFER takes effect when it returns and the GPU
 runs behind the CPU, so an unmap removed pages from under submits that
 were still executing. horizon_gpu states whose job the ordering is, in
 the imperative -- "The caller must ensure submits referencing the VA
 have retired" (horizon_gpu/vm.h) -- and nothing in this backend did it.
 It is not the unbind path, which is why fixing only that would have
 changed nothing. nvkmd_horizon_va_unbind is reached only from
 nvkmd_horizon_ctx_bind, whose four callers all use queue->bind_ctx,
 which nvk_queue.c creates only for a queue family carrying
 VK_QUEUE_SPARSE_BINDING_BIT -- and nvkmd_horizon_pdev.c sets
 has_sparse to false, so that family does not exist here and neither
 function runs. The reachable unmap is nvkmd_horizon_va_free, which
 tears its bindings down directly: every nvkmd_mem carries a VA of its
 own bound at offset 0, released by nvkmd_horizon_mem_free before the
 object is destroyed, so every vkFreeMemory that drops the last
 reference unmapped the address NVK bakes into every buffer device
 address, and vkDestroyImage and vkDestroyBuffer did the same through
 their plane and buffer VAs. Both paths now go through one deferral.
 Every live channel is waited on, not just one: two contexts exist on
 this device -- the queue's exec context and nvk_upload_queue's -- and
 either can have referenced the memory, so ordering against one of them
 would be the same bug, halved. The fences are snapshotted from nvkmd's
 own ctx->last_fence under a new per-context mutex rather than from
 horizon_gpu_channel_last_fence, because horizon_gpu_channel keeps no
 lock of its own -- channel.c reads chan->shadow_target unlocked while
 horizon_gpu_submit writes it -- and this runs on whichever thread
 called vkFreeMemory. Same number, no data race pushed down into
 horizon/.
 A lost channel counts as passed. A reached threshold on a faulted
 channel proves nothing about the work, because nvgpu's recovery
 force-increments the syncpoint, and a wait must keep reporting
 CHANNEL_LOST -- it does. But the question here is whether the GPU can
 still reach the address, and a lost channel never executes again.
 Deferring for ever instead would strand the mapping, the memory and
 the reservation for the life of the process, which is the leak patch
 0043 exists to prevent.
 Nothing is queued in a conformant application. VUID-vkFreeMemory-
 memory-00677 and VUID-vkDestroyImage-image-01000 both require every
 submitted command referring to the object to have completed before the
 call, so the fences are already signalled, the unmap happens inline and
 the reference is dropped inline, exactly as before. What is bought is
 that an application which does not obey them -- or a driver-internal
 free that races the GPU -- waits instead of faulting.
 The reaper is deliberately absent from nvkmd_horizon_ctx_exec.
 Completing a deferred unmap drops a memory reference, which can be the
 last one, which runs nvkmd_horizon_mem_free -> nvkmd_va_free -> a wait
 for the GPU. On the submit thread that would be the unconditional stall
 rejected design 6 forbids, arrived at sideways.
Evidence: HOST AND STATIC ONLY -- NO CONSOLE HAS RUN THIS, and the full
 cross link had not finished when this was committed.
 The defect is visible by reading: before this patch,
 `grep -n 'fence\|retire\|reap' nvkmd/horizon/nvkmd_horizon_va.c`
 returned nothing -- the file that unmaps had no reference to completion
 of any kind, while horizon_gpu/vm.h:95-98 requires one of its caller.
 The reachability that decides where the fix goes was read out of the
 tree at mesa-26.1.6 + the 77-patch series: nvkmd_horizon_pdev.c:435
 `.has_sparse = false`, nvk_physical_device.c:1634 derives
 VK_QUEUE_SPARSE_BINDING_BIT from it, nvk_queue.c:436 creates bind_ctx
 only under that bit, and all four nvkmd_ctx_bind callers
 (nvk_image.c:1837,1935,2005 and nvk_buffer.c:336) use queue->bind_ctx
 -- so nvkmd_horizon_ctx_bind and nvkmd_horizon_va_unbind do not run
 here at all, and nvkmd_horizon_va_free is the path that does.
 `scripts/run-host-tests.sh`: RESULT: PASS on all six suites, 132/132
 (h_align 20/20, h_va_space 21/21, h_syncpt_math 30/30, h_cmds 39/39,
 h_status 14/14, h_log 8/8) -- a no-regression gate, not coverage of
 this code, which lives in Mesa and cannot be reached from there.
 check-layering, check-no-abs-paths, check-history-intact and
 check-mesa-test-parity all report OK.
Upstream: no -- every other backend gets this ordering from the kernel's
 VM_BIND queue, which this platform does not have.
